## Proof

**Answer-leak check:** The problem statement contains only the problem, no solution sketch, formalization notes, or embedded answers. No leak detected.

---

**Claim.** The sequences $\mathfrak{c}=\langle c_0,c_1,c_2,\dots\rangle\in\mathbb{Z}^{\mathbb{N}}$ for which $I_{\mathfrak{c}}$ is an ideal of $\mathbb{Z}[x]$ are exactly
$$c_i = c_0\,p^{\,i}\quad(i\ge 0)$$
for some $c_0\in\mathbb{Z}$ and $p\in\mathbb{Z}$. Equivalently, $\mathfrak{c}=c_0\langle 1,p,p^2,p^3,\dots\rangle$. When $c_0\neq 0$ the ideal is $I_{\mathfrak{c}}=(x-p)$; when $c_0=0$ (all $c_i=0$) the ideal is $I_{\mathfrak{c}}=\mathbb{Z}[x]$.

---

### Setup

Define the additive group homomorphism (of free abelian groups)
$$\phi_{\mathfrak{c}}:\mathbb{Z}[x]\longrightarrow\mathbb{Z},\qquad \phi_{\mathfrak{c}}\!\left(\sum a_i x^i\right)=\sum a_i\,c_i,$$
so that $I_{\mathfrak{c}}=\ker\phi_{\mathfrak{c}}$. Since the kernel of any group homomorphism is a subgroup, $I_{\mathfrak{c}}$ is always an additive subgroup of $\mathbb{Z}[x]$. It remains to determine when it is closed under multiplication by arbitrary elements of $\mathbb{Z}[x]$.

**Closure under integer scalars is automatic:** if $f=\sum a_i x^i\in I_{\mathfrak{c}}$ then $\sum a_i c_i=0$, so $\phi_{\mathfrak{c}}(nf)=n\sum a_i c_i=0$.

**Closure under $x$ is the key condition.** Define a second homomorphism
$$\psi_{\mathfrak{c}}:\mathbb{Z}[x]\longrightarrow\mathbb{Z},\qquad \psi_{\mathfrak{c}}\!\left(\sum a_i x^i\right)=\sum a_i\,c_{i+1},$$
so that $\psi_{\mathfrak{c}}(f)=\phi_{\mathfrak{c}}(xf)$. Then $I_{\mathfrak{c}}$ is closed under multiplication by $x$ iff
$$\ker\phi_{\mathfrak{c}}\subseteq\ker\psi_{\mathfrak{c}}.\tag{$\star$}$$
Since $\mathbb{Z}[x]$ is generated as a ring by $\mathbb{Z}$ and $x$, conditions (automatic integer closure) $+$ ($\star$) are equivalent to $I_{\mathfrak{c}}$ being an ideal.

---

### Analyzing condition $(\star)$

**Fact (factorization through the image).** *For homomorphisms of abelian groups $\phi:A\to B$, $\psi:A\to C$ with $\ker\phi\subseteq\ker\psi$, there exists a unique homomorphism $\alpha:\operatorname{im}\phi\to C$ with $\psi=\alpha\circ\phi$.*

*Proof.* Define $\alpha(\phi(a))=\psi(a)$; this is well defined since $\phi(a)=\phi(a')\Rightarrow a-a'\in\ker\phi\subseteq\ker\psi\Rightarrow\psi(a)=\psi(a')$. ∎

Applying this to $(\star)$: there is a homomorphism $\alpha:\operatorname{im}\phi_{\mathfrak{c}}\to\mathbb{Z}$ with $\psi_{\mathfrak{c}}=\alpha\circ\phi_{\mathfrak{c}}$, i.e.,
$$c_{i+1}=\psi_{\mathfrak{c}}(x^i)=\alpha(\phi_{\mathfrak{c}}(x^i))=\alpha(c_i)\qquad\text{for all }i\ge 0.\tag{1}$$

Now $\operatorname{im}\phi_{\mathfrak{c}}$ is a subgroup of $\mathbb{Z}$, hence $\operatorname{im}\phi_{\mathfrak{c}}=d\mathbb{Z}$ for a unique $d\ge 0$ (namely $d=\gcd(c_0,c_1,c_2,\dots)$, with $d=0$ iff all $c_i=0$).

**Case 1: $d=0$ (all $c_i=0$).** Then $I_{\mathfrak{c}}=\mathbb{Z}[x]$, an ideal. This is $c_0=0$ in the claimed form.

**Case 2: $d>0$.** Every homomorphism $\alpha:d\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}$ is determined by $\alpha(d)=m$ for some $m\in\mathbb{Z}$, giving $\alpha(kd)=km$. Since $c_i\in d\mathbb{Z}$, write $c_i=k_i\,d$; then (1) gives
$$c_{i+1}=\alpha(c_i)=k_i\,m=\frac{m}{d}\,c_i.$$
Setting $r:=m/d\in\mathbb{Q}$, we obtain the **recurrence**
$$c_{i+1}=r\,c_i\quad\text{for all }i\ge 0,\qquad\text{hence}\quad c_i=r^{\,i}\,c_0.\tag{2}$$

Write $r=p/q$ in lowest terms ($q>0$, $\gcd(p,q)=1$). Then $c_i=(p/q)^i c_0$. For every $c_i$ to be an integer, we need $q^i\mid c_0$ for all $i\ge 0$.

- If $c_0=0$: (2) gives $c_i=0$ for all $i$, contradicting $d>0$. So this subcase does not arise.
- If $c_0\neq 0$: $|c_0|$ is a fixed positive integer, so $q^i\mid c_0$ for all $i$ forces $q=1$ (otherwise $q^i$ exceeds $|c_0|$ for large $i$). Thus $r=p\in\mathbb{Z}$, and
$$c_i = c_0\,p^{\,i}\quad\text{for all }i\ge 0.$$

---

### Converse (sufficiency)

Let $c_i=c_0\,p^i$ for $c_0\in\mathbb{Z}$, $p\in\mathbb{Z}$.

- **$c_0=0$:** all $c_i=0$, $I_{\mathfrak{c}}=\mathbb{Z}[x]$, an ideal. ✓
- **$c_0\neq 0$:** $\phi_{\mathfrak{c}}(f)=c_0\sum a_i p^i=c_0\,f(p)$, so
$$I_{\mathfrak{c}}=\{f\in\mathbb{Z}[x]:c_0\,f(p)=0\}=\{f:f(p)=0\}=\ker(\operatorname{ev}_p)=(x-p),$$
a (maximal) ideal of $\mathbb{Z}[x]$. ✓

---

### Conclusion

The infinite integer sequences $\mathfrak{c}=\langle c_0,c_1,c_2,\dots\rangle$ for which $I_{\mathfrak{c}}$ is an ideal of $\mathbb{Z}[x]$ are precisely

$$\boxed{\,c_i = c_0\,p^{\,i}\text{ for all }i\ge 0,\quad c_0\in\mathbb{Z},\; p\in\mathbb{Z}\,}.$$

Equivalently: either $\mathfrak{c}$ is the zero sequence (giving $I_{\mathfrak{c}}=\mathbb{Z}[x]$), or $\mathfrak{c}=c_0\langle 1,p,p^2,\dots\rangle$ with $c_0\neq 0$ (giving $I_{\mathfrak{c}}=(x-p)$, the kernel of evaluation at the integer $p$).

